					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606466841" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1509702832 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Nov 2017 09:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3625402">
									

<p>I've been thinking about this quite a lot, and I've finally deciphered why I didn't end with the first log. I think that the &quot;pops up in someone else's brain&quot; idea, while it has some punch (and clearly has the most punch for some readers), is probably the &quot;expected twist&quot; for this site. It is one obvious direction to take the anomaly, based on a wormhole within a brain, it is horrific but a little bit jokey - it fits very well within the site aesthetic all round (it is exactly how the Series I version of this skip would have ended, I think - maybe I would add that the unfortunate Mr Miller had taken a pill labelled &quot;increase your brain matter&quot;).</p>
<p>I wanted this one to zag slightly when the reader expected it to zig. That's why I wanted the brain-to-brain test to be the first - to get it out of the way, and leave the reader a little more in the dark about where the skip was going. And I wanted the ending to be horrible and inexplicable, with essentially no jokiness at all.</p>
<p>It may not be the right answer, but I thought it was worth explaining why I made the writing choice.</p>

